the family of sir david amos pours in to  read tributes and to remember  mourning the loss of a husband and  father  in westminster mps mourning the loss of  a colleague and friend  gathered together in quiet thought and  prayer may the bright memory of his rich  life  ever outshine the tragic manner of his  death  let us keep silence  i now call the prime minister  we've lost a dear friend and colleague  and julia and her children  have lost a loving husband  and devoted father  nothing i  or anyone else can say will lessen the  pain  the grief the anger  they must feel at this darkest of times  a tenacious campaigner who achieves so  much in his career sir david's best  known decades-long campaign completed  only after his death  he never once witnessed any achievement  by any resident of south end  that could not somehow be cited in his  bid to secure city status  for that distinguished time  i am happy to announce  that her majesty has agreed  the south end  will be accorded the city status it so  clearly deserves  sir david an example to follow for any  mp from any party  even as a political opponent he was a  man and a politician we can all learn so  much from because david held his beliefs  passionately  but gently  and i believe that not only can we learn  from that  but we have a duty  to learn from that  civility matters  and it matters in politics  mp share in their fond memories of sir  david but reflecting too on a politics  that has become far too toxic  he was appalled by what he called the  vile misogynistic abuse  which female mps had to endure online  and he told me very recently that he  wanted something done about  all of us can do better  not to feed into that corrosive culture  all of us have been a victim of it and  every single one of us  have a responsibility to put an end to  it  but the death of an mp in such tragic  circumstances taking us back the sister  of murdered mp joe cox nearly overcome  with emotion  i blocked out much of what happened when  joe was murdered  but i remember very clearly the moment i  took the phone call saying she had been  attacked  i remember physically trembling  and the visceral pain that overtook me  and it breaks my heart to think that  another family has had to experience  that phone call  and the nightmare which follows  is a roller coaster of deep trauma that  no one should have to experience  today is about david and his family  along with his staff  colleagues and the community he served  so well  the service he gave  and the support we should show all of  them in the coming days weeks and months  it is up to us to make sure that we do  because i know more than most that they  will need it and the powerful difference  it will make to them  after joining together in the commons  mps joined together again in church  for a service to commemorate sir david's  life and to mourn his loss  public service in politics  is a sacrifice that should be honored  and respected  even when differences of opinion  run very deep indeed  david showed that  divisions put aside mp's united in grief  it has taken the death of sir david amis  to remind them that they have far more  in common than what drives them apart  beth rigby sky news westminster
 

